i='TCS-2675';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='COLT 2022 open-problem note: Sections 1 and 3, especially the comparison immediately preceding Open Problem 2. The performance measure is limsup_T T/log(1/p_{μ,T}), not finite-budget error or cumulative regret.'
refs.extend([dict(id='bernoulli2024',title='On Universally Optimal Algorithms for A/B Testing',authors='Po-An Wang, Kaito Ariu, Alexandre Proutiere',year=2024,url='https://proceedings.mlr.press/v235/wang24c.html',locator='ICML 2024: the Bernoulli model, Definition 2.1 and Theorem 2.2 read. With two Bernoulli arms, any algorithm uniformly no worse than equal allocation has the same asymptotic performance on every instance; strict improvement anywhere is impossible.'),dict(id='gaussian2025',title='Admissibility of Completely Randomized Trials: A Large-Deviation Approach',authors='Guido Imbens, Chao Qin, Stefan Wager',year=2025,url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.05329',locator='arXiv v1 submitted 5 June 2025, PDF dated 12 June 2025: Assumption 1, Definitions 1–2, Proposition 1 and Theorem 5 read. For K≥3 Gaussian arms with common variance, proves strict uniform improvement over equal allocation. A one-page abstract appeared at EC 2025; the full proof source checked is the preprint.')])
issue='The 2022 note does not specify the reward-distribution class or a mathematical meaning of “other than uniform sampling”. Later work interprets the substantive target as weak domination on every instance and strict improvement on at least one, and obtains different answers for two Bernoulli arms and at least three equal-variance Gaussian arms. Any residual open benchmark must name the reward family, number-of-arms scope and strictness criterion rather than presenting this historical broad question as uniformly unresolved.'
save(i,dict(title='Can adaptive best-arm identification dominate uniform sampling?',formal='In fixed-budget best-arm identification, does there exist one sampling and recommendation policy whose asymptotic error performance is no worse than uniform sampling on every instance in the intended reward class, and is substantively different from that baseline? The original note leaves the reward class and meaning of “other” unspecified. Later results answer the strict-domination interpretation negatively for two Bernoulli arms and positively for at least three Gaussian arms of common variance.',definitions='An instance μ=(μ₁,…,μ_K) consists of K≥2 reward distributions with finite means θᵢ and a unique best arm i*(μ)=argmaxᵢθᵢ. At round t a policy chooses Iₜ using the known budget T, its internal randomness and previous choices and observations, then observes an independent reward from μ_{Iₜ}. After exactly T observations it recommends an arm Ĩ_T. The policy cannot depend on the unknown instance. Write p^A_{μ,T}=Pr_μ(Ĩ_T≠i*(μ)), including reward and policy randomness. The source compares H_A(μ)=limsup_{T→∞} T/log(1/p^A_{μ,T}), with smaller values better; equivalently it compares e_A(μ)=liminf_{T→∞}−T^{−1}log p^A_{μ,T}, with larger values better. Use the extended conventions 1/0=∞ and 1/∞=0. The uniform baseline used by the follow-up papers allocates approximately T/K observations to each arm with counts differing by at most one and returns an arm of largest empirical mean, breaking ties by a fixed index rule. It is not independent random allocation with fluctuating counts. The substantive domination criterion in those papers is ∀μ∈S: e_A(μ)≥e_U(μ), with strict inequality for some μ∈S. The set S, fixed before choosing the policy, is essential. The 2025 result uses S={N(θ₁,σ²),…,N(θ_K,σ²):θ has a unique maximum} for a fixed common σ²>0; the 2024 negative result uses two Bernoulli rewards with means in (0,1) and unequal means. These are distinct instance classes.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='For a specified instance class and nontrivial domination criterion, establish one policy with the required inequality on all instances and the required strict improvement, or prove no such policy exists. An instance-specific policy that knows μ, a better finite-budget error on selected instances, or a fixed-confidence guarantee is insufficient. The already established Bernoulli and Gaussian cases must not be relabeled as open; a remaining target requires a separately justified model.',context_blocks=[b('The budget is fixed before observations','The experiment must stop after T samples. It cannot keep sampling until a chosen confidence level is reached, so fixed-confidence optimality theorems do not automatically answer this comparison.', 'primary'),b('Uniform improvement is weaker than instancewise optimality','A policy need only beat a particular baseline uniformly. It need not attain the best conceivable exponent at each instance, and impossibility of such universal optimality does not by itself rule out domination of uniform sampling.', 'primary'),b('Two Bernoulli arms give a negative answer to strict domination','The ICML 2024 theorem shows that a policy that is asymptotically no worse everywhere than equal allocation cannot strictly improve its exponent on even one instance in this class.', 'bernoulli2024'),b('At least three equal-variance Gaussian arms give a positive answer','The 2025 preprint proves that certain adaptive policies have strictly larger error exponents on every unique-best-arm instance. The uniform baseline has exponent Δ_min²/(4Kσ²), where Δ_min is the difference between the largest and second-largest means.', 'gaussian2025'),b('Simple regret and error probability agree at the exponent scale','For a fixed unique-best-arm instance, simple regret lies between Δ_min·p_{μ,T} and Δ_max·p_{μ,T}, with fixed positive gaps. Thus the Gaussian paper’s simple-regret exponent also gives the error-probability comparison in the original question.', 'gaussian2025'),b('What remains to specify',issue)],why='Clarifies whether adaptive experimentation can uniformly improve a robust equal-allocation baseline, separating benefits of adaptivity from unattainable instancewise optimality.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2022',text='Qin poses uniform domination of uniform sampling as Open Problem 2, using fixed-budget asymptotic error performance.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2024',text='ICML 2024 rules out strict uniform domination for two Bernoulli arms.',citation='bernoulli2024'),dict(date='2025-06-05',text='A preprint proves strict uniform domination for K≥3 Gaussian arms with common variance and identifies this as an affirmative answer in that model; an abstract appeared at EC 2025.',citation='gaussian2025')],status='uncertain',status_note='The source performance criterion and both later model-specific answers were checked, including the Gaussian equal-variance assumption and the equivalence of simple-regret and error exponents. The 2025 full result was checked as a preprint, not a peer-reviewed full article. No unqualified open status is assigned to a question whose answer and residual scope depend on unspecified reward assumptions.',model_self_contained=False,requires_context=True,statement_review=dict(reviewed_on=DATE,status='needs_specification',remaining_issue=issue,notes=['Recovered the fixed-budget exponent comparison and distinguished the two later model-specific answers.']),importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A basic comparison of adaptive versus equal-allocation experiments under large-deviation error guarantees. Existing numerical score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note='Restored the asymptotic comparison, recommendation error and equal-count baseline. Incorporated both the published Bernoulli impossibility and the Gaussian preprint’s positive theorem, without silently specializing the original unspecified class.',working_summary=dict(sentences=['The source compares adaptive and uniform sampling for selecting the best arm after a fixed budget.','Performance is measured by the asymptotic exponential rate of recommendation error on each instance.','Two Bernoulli arms admit no strict uniform improvement over equal allocation.','For at least three Gaussian arms of common variance, a 2025 preprint proves strict improvement on every instance.','The original unspecified reward class and nontriviality criterion must be fixed before declaring a residual open problem.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','bernoulli2024','gaussian2025'])),notes=['Recovered the exact limsup/liminf performance convention and uniform instance quantifier.','Defined the fixed-budget sequential observation model and equal-count baseline.','Verified the distinct Bernoulli and Gaussian answers, including reward assumptions.','Separated preprint evidence and a published abstract from full-paper publication; kept residual model choices explicit.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://sigecom.org/proceedings25.html'],state='improved_unfinished')
